.\" $Revision: 1.7 $
.\" $Log: hashpipe.1,v $
.\" Revision 1.7  2026/09/05 02:09:20  dlr
.\" Document -N, the type-table listing option.
.\"
.\" Prints index, name, hashcat modes, flags and the self-test vector as TSV, one
.\" row per type. It exists so HASH_TYPES.md can be generated from the binary
.\" rather than parsed out of hashpipe.c, where rules such as HTF_UC are applied
.\" at render time and an external parser has to reimplement them.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.6  2026/09/04 17:16:45  dlr
.\" Document -c, which verifies each line against its own leading TYPE label and rejects what does not match. Covers the failure modes, deliberately loud where -m is silent: an unknown label is fatal with INVALID LABEL on stderr and exit 2, a line with no label is treated the same, and a pair that simply does not verify goes to -E with the run still completing 0. Records that the xNN suffix sets the depth so -i does not bound it, and that user-defined types are restricted the same way. Adds a Why this mode exists subsection giving the reasoning: verifying is one hash, identifying may be hundreds, and after an mdxfind run the type is already on the line, measured over the full generated corpus at over an hour against 98.7 seconds with both resolving all 7234771 lines. Notes the trade of recall for strictness and the two pass recipe that recovers it. EXIT STATUS now covers 2 and distinguishes an invalid label from a line that merely failed to verify. TH to 1.190.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.5  2026/08/31 18:15:06  dlr
.\" Document that -m selection is strict by design: it is a filter, not a hint. A type not listed is never tried and there is no fallback to auto-detection unless auto appears in the spec. Records the failure mode, which is the operationally important part: an omitted type sends the line to stderr as unresolved, reported exactly like a failed verify, with nothing distinguishing a wrong type selection from a wrong password. Same shape of silent negative as the -L cost guard, and it wants the same discipline - append auto when the type is uncertain, and when measuring coverage over a bundled type list. Waffle decision 2026-08-31, after weighing making -m a preference instead; strict is the better fit for how the tool is actually used.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.4  2026/08/29 18:06:35  dlr
.\" Three updates for hashpipe.c 1.114. The -X -p mode now decodes $HEX[...], closing the divergence with the standalone hx command that the page previously recorded as current behaviour; shows the two invocations agreeing, with the pre-1.114 behaviour kept as one sentence since it explains older output. Makes -s dual use explicit: one handler assigns both statfile and hx_salt, the meaning is decided by mode alone, both cannot be had in one invocation and there is no diagnostic if the other meaning was expected; the hx-mode entry cross-references it. Bumps TH to 1.114. Page covers 25 of 25.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.3  2026/08/29 17:35:32  dlr
.\" Two additions. Show the $HEX[] wrapper on output with a verified pair of equivalent invocations, plain colon-bearing password and hex-wrapped, both yielding the same line, and state that the password is everything after the preceding field so a colon parses. Separately, record that -X mode does NOT decode $HEX[] on -p, unlike the standalone hx command running the same language: a wrapped argument is hashed as the literal text. Verified -- hashpipe -X md5(pass) -p $HEX[616c706861] returns md5 of the literal string, while hx -p with the same argument returns md5 of alpha.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.2  2026/08/29 17:16:00  dlr
.\" Remove the redundant .PP immediately after .SH EXAMPLES. mandoc reports it as "skipping paragraph macro: PP after SH"; groff does not flag it at all, which is how it survived a groff-only check. macOS and the BSDs render with mandoc, so mandoc is what most readers of these pages actually see. No rendered output changes.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 16:30:16  dlr
.\" Initial revision: hashpipe(1) man page. Covers all 24 getopt options including the 11 absent from the built-in usage text (-L -G -X -F -p -S -P -u -D -Y -J). Documents the -L silent-decline hazard on expensive types, the -J exact-match output mode selector and HASHPIPE_OUTPUT, and the hx expression mode where -F is a script file rather than a hash file.
.\"
.TH HASHPIPE 1 "2026-09-04" "hashpipe 1.190" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
hashpipe \- verify and identify hash/password pairs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hashpipe
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file " ...]"
.br
.B hashpipe
.B \-T
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hashpipe
.BI \-X " expression"
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B hashpipe
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hashpipe
answers one question per input line: does this password produce this hash,
and under which algorithm?  It is a verifier and identifier, not a searcher.
Where
.BR mdxfind (1)
runs a candidate list against a hash list,
.B hashpipe
takes candidate/hash pairs that are already paired up and confirms or
refutes them.
.PP
Input is read from the named
.IR file s,
or from standard input if none are given, as lines of the form
.PP
.RS
.RI [ TYPE [\fBx\fINN\fR]\  ] hash [\fB:\fIsalt\fR] \fB:\fIpassword
.RE
.PP
The leading type label is optional.  When it is absent
.B hashpipe
identifies the type itself by trying every registered algorithm whose
digest length matches, then \(em on the
.I hard pass
\(em by iterating the unsalted, non-composed types from depth 2 up to the
.B \-i
limit.
.PP
Verified lines are written to standard output in the same shape, with the
type and iteration depth filled in.  Lines that could not be resolved are
written unchanged to standard error.  The two streams are the result: a
clean split of solved from unsolved.
.PP
The password is everything after the field that precedes it, so a password
may itself contain a colon and still parse.  On output such a password is
wrapped in
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
so the emitted line stays unambiguous, and the wrapped form is equally
accepted on input.  These two invocations are equivalent, and both produce
the same line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ echo '48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:pass:word' | hashpipe \-m e1
MD5x01 48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]

$ echo '48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]' \e
      | hashpipe \-m e1
MD5x01 48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B hashpipe
is CPU-only.  It has no GPU path and is not intended to acquire one.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Type selection and search depth"
.TP
.BI \-m " spec"
Restrict the search to the types in
.IR spec ,
a comma-separated list of bare
.BR hashcat (1)
mode numbers,
.BI e N
internal indices, and
.BI e N \- e M
ranges.  The token
.B auto
anywhere in the list re-enables full auto-detection as a fallback after the
listed types fail.  Restricting the type set is the single most effective
way to speed up a large run.
.IP
Selection is
.I strict
by design:
.B \-m
is a filter, not a hint.  A type that is not listed is never tried, and there is
no fallback to auto-detection unless
.B auto
appears in
.IR spec .
.IP
The failure mode follows from that.  If the correct type is not in the list, the
line goes to standard error as unresolved \(em reported exactly like a failed
verify, with nothing to distinguish
.I "the right type was not selected"
from
.IR "the password does not match" .
This is the same shape of silent negative that
.B \-L
produces, and it wants the same discipline: append
.B auto
whenever the type is uncertain, and whenever measuring coverage over a bundled
type list, where an omitted type under-reports silently rather than erroring.
.TP
.B \-c
Verify each line against its own leading
.I TYPE
label and nothing else.  Detection is not run and there is no fallback: a pair
that does not verify as the type its label names is written to
.B \-E
as unresolved.
.IP
The label is the one
.BR mdxfind (1)
already emits \(em its output is
.I "TYPE hash[:salt]:password"
\(em so a solution file feeds straight back in.  Tools that derive the type
from a filename produce the same shape; the contest intake script builds it
from the solution file's extension.
.IP
This is the mirror image of
.BR \-m .
Where
.B \-m
applies one filter to the whole run,
.B \-c
takes the type from each line, so a mixed file of many types costs one verify
per line rather than a search.  Where an omitted type under
.B \-m
produces a silent negative,
.B \-c
is deliberately loud: a label naming a type this build does not know is
.I fatal.
The offending line is printed to standard error as
.IP
.RS
.B "INVALID LABEL: " \fIline\fP
.RE
.IP
and the process exits non-zero (2) immediately, on the reasoning that the
producer's type vocabulary and this build's disagree, so every following line
is equally unjudgeable.  A line carrying no label at all is treated the same
way.
.IP
The
.I xNN
suffix sets the iteration depth, so
.B \-i
does not bound it: a label of
.I x1000000
performs a million rounds because the label asked for them.  This mode is an
explicit verifier, and the cost is the operator's to choose.
.IP
User-defined types are restricted the same way.  A label naming a user type
tries only that type, and a label naming a built-in type does not try user
types at all \(em without which a mislabelled line could still be re-identified
as something else, which is the one thing this mode exists to prevent.  A user
label with no user definition loaded is an invalid label and therefore fatal.
.TP
.BI \-i " count"
Maximum iteration depth for the hard pass.  Default 128.
.TP
.BI \-q " count"
Iteration step size for types that carry their own internal iteration
function.  Default 128.
.TP
.BI \-L " seconds"
Maximum estimated seconds for a single verify.  Default 1000.
.IP
A verify whose estimated cost exceeds this limit is
.I skipped,
and a skipped verify is reported exactly like a failed one: the line goes to
standard error as unresolved and the run reports nothing found.  There is no
distinguishing diagnostic.  Memory-hard and high-round types \(em scrypt,
Argon2, high-cost bcrypt, and anything with a parsed cost far above its
benchmark cost \(em routinely trip the default and need
.B \-L
raised explicitly.
.IP
The tell is wall-clock time.  If a verify of an expensive hash returns in
about 0.00 seconds, it did not run.  Time a single candidate before
believing any negative result on a slow type.
.BR mdxfind (1)
has no such guard, so its negative results on these types are real.
.TP
.BI \-t " count"
Worker thread count.  Defaults to the number of CPUs.
.SS "Why this mode exists"
.PP
Verifying a pair is cheap; finding which of a thousand-odd types produced it is
not.  Auto-detection must try candidate types in turn, and for an ambiguous
digest width that includes expensive ones, so the cost per line is a search
rather than a single hash.  When the type is already known \(em and after an
.BR mdxfind (1)
run it always is, because the label is right there on the line \(em that search
is pure waste.
.PP
Measured on the full generated regression corpus, 7,234,771 lines across 1,020
types on one machine:
.PP
.RS
.PD 0
.TP 16
auto\-detect
more than 1 hour
.TP 16
.B \-c
98.7 seconds
.PD
.RE
.PP
Both resolve all 7,234,771 lines.  Per type the gap is wider still \(em an
iterated salted type measured 2.41 seconds identifying against 0.01 seconds
when the type was known, about 240 to 1 \(em because the saving scales with how
many candidates a shape admits and how costly the wrong ones are.
.PP
The trade is recall for speed and strictness.  A mislabelled but otherwise
correct solution is rejected rather than recovered.  Where that matters, run
.B \-c
first and re-run only its rejects without it: the second pass sees a few
hundred lines instead of millions, so it costs almost nothing and gets both.
.SS "Output routing"
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Append verified results to
.IR file .
Default is standard output.
.TP
.BI \-O " file"
As
.BR \-o ,
but truncate
.I file
first.
.TP
.BI \-e " file"
Append unresolved lines to
.IR file .
Default is standard error.
.TP
.BI \-E " file"
As
.BR \-e ,
but truncate first.
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
Append run statistics to
.IR file .
.IP
.B \-s
is
.IR "dual use" .
A single option handler assigns its argument to both the statistics file and
the hx salt, and which one takes effect depends only on the mode: in a normal
verification run it is a filename, and in hx expression mode \(em
.B \-X
or
.B \-F
\(em it is the value bound to the
.B salt
variable.  It is listed again under
.B "hx expression mode"
below for that reason.  There is no way to have both in one invocation, and
no diagnostic if you expect the other meaning.
.TP
.BI \-J " 0|1|2"
Output format.
.B 0
is mdxfind format,
.B 1
is John the Ripper format for input that arrived in John format, and
.B 2
is John format wherever a John equivalent exists.  The argument is matched
exactly; anything else is a fatal error rather than a silent fallback to
mode 0.  When a selected John mode has no label table compiled in,
.B hashpipe
says so on standard error and emits mdxfind format.
.SS "Self-test and benchmark"
.TP
.B \-T
Run the built-in self-tests over every registered type and report pass and
fail counts.  This is the first thing to run after a build.
.TP
.B \-G
As
.BR \-T ,
but also emit the generated example vectors.
.TP
.B \-N
Print the type table as tab-separated values: index, name, hashcat modes
(or
.BR n/a ),
flag letters, and the self-test vector.  One row per type, with a leading
comment line naming the build.  This is meant to be consumed rather than
read: it is what
.I gen-hash-types.sh
turns into
.IR HASH_TYPES.md .
The vector is emitted exactly as
.B \-T
verifies it, so a passing self-test is proof that every row is correct for the
build that produced it.  Types that share a compute function with another type
and carry no vector of their own print an empty final field.
.TP
.B \-B
Benchmark every registered type.
.TP
.BI \-b " spec"
Benchmark only the types in
.IR spec ,
using the same syntax as
.BR \-m .
.SS "hx expression mode"
These options turn
.B hashpipe
into a hash calculator driven by the
.BR hx (1)
expression language.  In this mode
.B hashpipe
computes and prints; it reads no hash file and performs no comparison.
.TP
.BI \-X " expression"
Evaluate
.I expression
against each password.
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Read the hx program from
.I file
instead of the command line.  Note that this is
.I not
a hash-file option; it does not correspond to
.BR "mdxfind \-F" .
.TP
.BI \-p " password"
Evaluate for this single password.  Without
.BR \-p ,
passwords are read one per line from standard input.
.IP
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
is decoded before use, so a password containing a colon, a control character
or a high-bit byte can be supplied on a command line.  This matches the
standalone
.BR hx (1)
command exactly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ hashpipe \-X 'md5(pass)' \-p '$HEX[616c706861]'
2c1743a391305fbf367df8e4f069f9f9

$ hx \-p '$HEX[616c706861]' 'md5(pass)'
2c1743a391305fbf367df8e4f069f9f9
.fi
.RE
.IP
Before hashpipe.c 1.114 this mode did not decode, and hashed the literal
text
.RB \(lq $HEX[...] \(rq
brackets and all, while the standalone command decoded.  The two now agree.
.TP
.BI \-s " salt"
Bind the
.B salt
variable.  Same option as the statistics file above; see there.
.TP
.BI \-S " salt2"
Bind the
.B salt2
variable.
.TP
.BI \-P " pepper"
Bind the
.B pepper
variable.
.TP
.BI \-u " user"
Bind the
.B user
variable.
.TP
.B \-D
Disassemble the compiled hx program and exit without running it.
.SS "Information"
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-Y
Load
.BR userdef.txt ,
print the load report, and exit.  Without
.B \-Y
the same file is loaded silently, so that pipe output is never polluted.
If
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
is unset, nothing is loaded and
.B \-Y
says so.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage summary followed by the full type table \(em internal
.BI e N
index, flags, name, and corresponding
.BR hashcat (1)
mode numbers.
.IP
Type flags are
.B s
salted,
.B u
uppercase-hex variant,
.B n
NTLM,
.B c
composed,
.B v
non-hex, and
.B V
has a dedicated verify function.
.SH "USER-DEFINED TYPES"
.B hashpipe
reads the same
.B userdef.txt
that
.BR mdxfind (1)
does, from the directory component of
.BR MDXFIND_CACHE ,
and identifies the types it declares.  With
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
unset, no user-defined types load at all and lines using them fall through
to unresolved with no diagnostic.
.PP
Bulk validation through user-defined types feeds an empty salt, so only
unsalted hx expressions can be validated this way.  The
.B \-X
and
.B \-F
options do not validate at all; they only compute.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HASHPIPE_OUTPUT
Selects the output format for callers that cannot pass
.BR \-J .
Same values.  An explicit
.B \-J
wins; when the environment variable is the one in effect,
.B hashpipe
announces it on standard error.
.TP
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
Path whose directory component holds
.BR userdef.txt .
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion, 1 on a usage or argument error.
.B \-V
exits 0.
.PP
.B \-T
exits 1 if any self-test failed.
.B \-c
exits 2 on an invalid label, immediately and before the remaining input is
read; that status distinguishes a broken type vocabulary from an ordinary
error, and a line failing to verify under
.B \-c
is not an error at all \(em it goes to
.B \-E
and the run completes 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Confirm a build is sound:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-T
.fi
.RE
.PP
Split a mixed pot file into verified and unverified halves:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-O verified.txt \-E unresolved.txt potfile.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Identify unlabelled pairs, restricted to three plausible types, with
auto-detect as a fallback:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-m e1,e8,1000,auto < pairs.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Verify an expensive memory-hard type.  Without
.BR \-L ,
this reports nothing found in no time at all:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-m e1001 \-L 100000 < cmiyc.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compute a compound hash for one password:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashpipe \-X 'sha1(md5(pass) . salt)' \-s deadbeef \-p password123
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR hx (1),
.BR mdsplit (1),
.BR getpass (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
